```java
// 1. Constructor injection (RECOMMENDED)
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo; // can be final!
    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}

// 2. Setter injection (optional dependencies)
@Service
public class NotificationService {
    private EmailService emailService;
    @Autowired(required = false)
    public void setEmailService(EmailService emailService) {
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }
}

// 3. Field injection (convenient but not recommended)
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repo; // no final, hard to test without Spring
}
```

```java
// Lombok @RequiredArgsConstructor removes boilerplate
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor  // generates constructor for all final fields
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;    // final!
    private final EmailService emailSvc;  // final!
    // No @Autowired needed
}
```

Setter injection is designed for cases where a dependency is **not required** for the core functionality of the class. For example, a `UserService` might optionally use a `CacheService` — if no cache bean is present, the service still works but without caching. Using `@Autowired(required=false)` on the setter means Spring injects it if available, skips if not.

```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    private CacheService cache; // optional

    @Autowired(required = false)
    public void setCache(CacheService cache) {
        this.cache = cache;
    }

    public User findUser(Long id) {
        if (cache != null) return cache.get(id);
        return repo.findById(id).orElse(null);
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class NotificationService {
    // Required: constructor injection
    private final UserRepository userRepo;
    private final MessageTemplateService templateService;

    public NotificationService(UserRepository repo, MessageTemplateService tmpl) {
        this.userRepo = repo;
        this.templateService = tmpl;
    }

    // Optional: setter injection
    private MetricsService metricsService;

    @Autowired(required = false)
    public void setMetricsService(MetricsService metricsService) {
        this.metricsService = metricsService;
    }
}
```

```java
// Field injection — testing is painful
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired private UserRepository repo; // can't pass in constructor
}
// Test requires: @SpringBootTest (slow) OR
// ReflectionTestUtils.setField(service, "repo", mockRepo);

// Constructor injection — testing is simple
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}
// Test: just new UserService(mock(UserRepository.class)) — clean and fast!
```

```java
// Spring 4.3+ — @Autowired optional on single constructor
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    private final EmailService emailSvc;

    // No @Autowired needed!
    public UserService(UserRepository repo, EmailService emailSvc) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.emailSvc = emailSvc;
    }
}

// Even cleaner with Lombok
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;    // auto-injected
    private final EmailService emailSvc;  // auto-injected
}
```
